{"answer":"The run refuted both parts of the question: it did not find that margin quality had deteriorated at these companies, and it did not find that revenue growth explained the apparent deterioration. Overall confidence was moderate at 0.6, but no claim was supported.

The first claim, that net margin had deteriorated over recent periods, was rejected. It had 2 supporting evidence items and 2 refuting evidence items, so the evidence split rather than converged. The second claim, that revenue growth was the explanation for the observed margin deterioration, was also rejected, with 1 supporting evidence item and 2 refuting evidence items.

The run used 7 experiments, including two edgar trend break analyses, two time-series trend analyses, two change-point detections, and a distribution summary. It stopped because of insufficient evidence, not because the question was fully settled. Two open questions remained: whether any margin decline was sustained across recent periods or just a one-off period, and whether the timing of revenue growth aligned with the margin movement."}

